prescription drug monitoring programs  are very powerful databases that  currently 49 states have implemented  it's a pretty new thing they've just  started in the past maybe five years or  so for most states although a handful of  states have had them for a couple of  decades the new thing about this is that  they're often web-based so as an  emergency physician I can see a patient  if I'm considering giving them a  medication like an opioid I can log on  to a state database and what it does is  it tells me all of the controlled  substance prescriptions those are  medications like benzodiazepines for  anxiety or pain medications like opioids  tells me every single prescription  they've had in the state and often in  surrounding states now as well too  this has been a game changer for us  there's so called doctor shopping where  patients go from hospital to hospital we  don't like that term but that's often  frequently what it's called and we've  seen a market decrease in that because  of the existence of prescription drug  monitoring programs and the reason is  that it's it becomes difficult to to  have multiple prescribers for your  medications so patients that go from  from a merchants department to emergency  department and get different  prescriptions now now have difficulty  with that frankly and that's that's  that's a good thing it's gotten to the  point where initially we were really  advocating for their for them to be  implemented and now the government has  realized how powerful these are and in  many states it's actually mandated so in  in the state of Massachusetts for  example if I want to prescribe a  prescription if I want to prescribe an  opioid I have to access the state  prescription drug monitoring program at  that point it's forced us to use it it's  it it's actually entered our workflow  which is a very good thing and probably  the most powerful part about this is  that if I'm seeing a patient that has a  profile that has lots of prescriptions  from lots of different providers I print  that out and I bring it to the bedside  and it starts a conversation why why do  you have so many providers why are you  getting so many prescriptions and that  can be a very powerful teachable moment  I think pharmacists play a very big role  number one because we're responsible for  dispensing the opioids so the opioids  have to come from somewhere and although  many of them are  found throughout a prescription usually  they are obtained by someone who had a  prescription from their doctor and they  got it at a pharmacy so it's our job as  pharmacists to make sure that every  prescription we fill is for a legitimate  medical purpose and it's written by a  doctor that is practicing within their  scope and so it is our job to prevent  any prescriptions being filled that  shouldn't basically having patients who  are in pain that need the medication get  it and the other patients that are  potentially abusing the drug or selling  it hopefully they they wouldn't be  getting it from the pharmacy we also  help avoid the opioid crisis by  maintaining an accurate inventory in the  pharmacy make sure there's no diversion  or stealing as you would call it and if  anyone calls and they ask hey how much  oxycodone do you have we don't give them  that information over the phone we keep  very strict inventory so there's many  ways including making sure that we check  the prescription monitoring program and  communicating with doctors if there's  any patient that's trying to fill  prescriptions early or anything that  seems out of the ordinary so there's  many ways that I feel that we can help  continue to decrease the crisis  sometimes we're faced with a situation  in the pharmacy where we do not fill an  opioid medication when the patient  brings the prescription even though the  doctor wrote it there's several reasons  that this may occur one of the most  common reasons is that it's too soon to  fill it we have a very strict policy as  far as when you can fill your controlled  substances to avoid anybody getting  early refills so typically if you filled  your prescription for your controlled  substance 30 days ago basically we would  never fill it sooner than 28 days from  that time so we have to make sure not to  fill any prescriptions  that are controlled Stubbs substances  and that's the number one reason why we  would deny a patient filling a  medication for a controlled substance  other reasons that we might not fill a  prescription is because of other red  flags that we see in their profile they  don't want to fill it through their  insurance for example which is a red  flag because everybody has insurance  it's required and basically if they're  saying I don't want to pay for it with  my insurance they're trying to avoid us  seeing them getting another prescription  most likely somewhere else where it did  go through the insurance so in this case  they're usually pharmacy shopping or  doctor shopping and in this case it's  easy now for us to check on the  prescription monitoring program online  and see where they filled their last  prescription how many they got are they  due for a refill it's our job basically  to make sure that if you have pain  medication you get it when you're due to  fill it not too soon not before it's due  but you should be taking it as  prescribed and in that case you  shouldn't need to get it early so if a  patient tells me that they're out  because they were taking it differently  than what the label said that concerns  me and I immediately contact the doctor  we we play a big role in keeping the  doctor in the loop because a lot of  information comes from the patient when  they're trying to get a prescription and  it's for instance too soon to be filled  we can also not fill a prescription if  after we talked to the doctor they say  hey I didn't know that they had this  other prescription they never told me  cancel the prescription so sometimes the  doctors do in fact cancel the  prescriptions that they already wrote  once they're informed about the other  prescriptions that they got these days  that's mostly avoided because they've  already seen before they wrote the  prescription by logging into the  prescription monitoring program what  they've got in the last time they got it  so that issue doesn't really fall in the  pharmacists anymore but sometimes you  know foster the cracks and a patient is  using more than one doctor and maybe  they're outside of the partners Network  and that's the situation where  you know we don't feel comfortable  filling the prescription but we keep the  doctor in the loop as well the med safe  program is basically a disposal unit  that sits outside the pharmacy at Mass  General and it allows patients at any  time of day to come and drop off their  medications whether it has a label on it  or it doesn't have a label they're not  sure what it is it doesn't matter as  long as it's not a syringe any type of  tablet at all can be disposed here and  it just gets incinerated and it's safely  handled by our employees at in the  pharmacy we just dispose of the box and  then the Med safe company incinerates it  so they don't need to worry about their  personal information being anywhere so  it's very safe and here at Mass General  it's so popular that we have to change  out the the unit about once a week so  there's a lot of medications out there  that are being dropped off so it's been  a very effective form of a drug  take-back every week we have to replace  the disposal unit and we send back about  50 pounds worth of drugs back to the  company to be incinerated just to give  you an idea of how many drugs are  actually dropped off every week here at  the hospital  it's it's a very significant amount and  it decreases the number of tablets that  are out on the in people's homes and  available for basically use and it could  be controls and non controls so it's not  just controlled substances that are  going in there it's it's any type of old  expired unused medication
 

